# Top 3 functions
note: based on number of excecuted instuctions, not time. 
Information form callgrind, build with -O2 -g -fno-inline

    1k long words:
    string_hash (42%), strcspn (15,1%), strcmp (6,3%)

    10k words:
    strcmp (25,7%), strcspn (13,2%), find_previous_entry (11,9%)

    16k long words:
    strcmp (50,3%), find_previous_entry (17,5%), string_eq (13,2%)
    
    small text:
    __tunables_init (14,4%), do_lookup_x (11,6%), _dl_relocate_object (6,9%)

For each input file, the top 3 functions vary. For a very small file, small.txt, the top three functions are all functions used to start upp the program. So the top three are niether our or library functions, but connected to ld. 

For the 1k-long-words file, our function string_hash dominates (42%!) since the words are very long, the hash function becomes expensive. the other two of the top three, strcspn and strcmp are library functions. In the 1k-long-words file, we only have 23 unique words, so what becomes expensive in this file, is looking at every symbol and chechking that none is a delimiter etc. Since we do not have many words, there will not be very many collisions and therefore only a few short chains. Strcmp has to go through every sign in the word until a delimiter is reached. strcmp is called through string_eq when find_previous_entry goes through a chain. Since we have few unique words, the function are called few times, but each call becomes more expensive. String hash still dominates though, since it has to go through the entrie word every time it is called. This cost O(word lenght) every time. 

For 10k words: 
For the 10k_words file, the library functions strcmp and strcspn dominates. In this file, we have 199 unique words and since we only have 17 buckets, there will be about 12 elements in each chain. For every search there will therefore be more comparisions done and therefore strcmp is the functions mostly used. strcspn seaches for the first occurence ina. string of any od the specified delimiters, and in this file, there are not quite enogh words for the amout of characters to not matter. The more words we have, the bigger percentage of the program lies in hash table. For this file, we are somewhere in the middle, where the wordcount is big enough for strcmp and outehr functions used to hansdle the hash table becomes important, but the hashing is not that much bugger that the tokenising. 

For the 16k_words file, strcmp, find_previous_entry and string_eq are the top three functions. For this file, we have 4370 unique words and only 17 buckets still, therefore there will be a lot of collisions and logn chains in each bucket. This means that every lookup and insert fucntions is oing to cost a lot more. 

# Conclusion:
For very small files, the startup of the program costs the most. For programs with a small amout of unique word but with very long words, the hashing and looking for where the word ends dominates. Having to go through every element in each word in order to hash it cost a lot. For files with quite long words but also quite many, we find some kind of middle ground. Where going through each word and looking for where the word ends still costs a lot, but the number of unique words is significant enoght that handling the chaining starts to matter a lot more. For even bigger files with even more unique words the chianing matters even more. We then have more unique words but the same amout of buckets. This means that there will be even more collisions and therefor also longer chains to search when using the lookup and insert functions. Here, the cost of going through each symbokl in the eods becomes less that the cost of looking for the right words.

